## The House of Cards: why paint a child's game?
sources:
    - id: nga
      title: The House of Cards (1937.1.90)
      authors: National Gallery of Art, Washington
      year: 2024
      finding: Probably 1737, oil on canvas, 82.2 × 66 cm; jack of hearts in the drawer; in Catherine II's Hermitage by 1774, bought by Andrew Mellon in 1931
    - id: ngl
      title: The House of Cards — catalogue entry (Wine, 2018)
      authors: Humphrey Wine, The National Gallery, London
      year: 2018
      finding: Four versions (Waddesdon, Louvre, Washington, London); the boy treats a card only as a 'brick'; moralising verses on the prints; Chardin depicts a process rather than a moral
      url: https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/catalogues/wine-2018/the-house-of-cards
    - id: utp
      title: House of Cards (Washington version) — Chardin
      authors: Utpictura18, Aix-Marseille Université
      year: 2024
      finding: The engraving's verse asking which is more solid, our projects or the child's castles; Catherine II's collection from 1774
      url: https://utpictura18.univ-amu.fr/en/notice/16573-house-of-cards-washington-version-chardin
    - id: ngachardin
      title: '18th-Century France: Chardin and Portraiture'
      authors: National Gallery of Art, Washington
      year: 2024
      finding: After a fellow artist's gibe about his lowly genre, Chardin began about 1730 to paint figures, mostly women and children

    card title "The House of Cards"

    image art cites ["nga","ngl"] credit "Chardin, probably 1737. National Gallery of Art, Washington. Public domain." x 296 y 345 height 640 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Jean_Sim%C3%A9on_Chardin%2C_The_House_of_Cards%2C_probably_1737%2C_NGA_97.jpg/1920px-Jean_Sim%C3%A9on_Chardin%2C_The_House_of_Cards%2C_probably_1737%2C_NGA_97.jpg look screen regions.blank [0.213,0.832,0.097,0.067] regions.boy [0.4,0.18,0.56,0.8] regions.builder [0,0.2,0.66,0.6] regions.card_wall [0,0.635,0.22,0.15] regions.cheek [0.47,0.33,0.07,0.07] regions.coins [0.375,0.805,0.175,0.035] regions.counter [0.05,0.79,0.14,0.035] regions.cuff [0.442,0.685,0.05,0.08] regions.drawer [0.1,0.82,0.38,0.15] regions.drawer_front [0.1,0.89,0.38,0.077] regions.face [0.45,0.24,0.17,0.18] regions.folded [0.25,0.61,0.11,0.095] regions.hands [0.2,0.61,0.27,0.16] regions.jack [0.125,0.825,0.095,0.07] regions.room [0.1,0.15,0.8,0.7] regions.signature [0.54,0.918,0.095,0.035] regions.table [0,0.6,0.58,0.3]

A boy leans over a card table, and our question is simple: why would anyone paint this?
    camera on ["art:room"] zoom 1.4 duration 6

    camera reset true

This is The House of Cards by Jean-Siméon Chardin, from about 1737; his si (@highlight art:signature effect detail@)gnature is on the table.

He painted the subject several times, and this version hangs in Washington.

    portrait chardin x 780 y 400 width 190 of "Jean-Siméon Chardin" cameo true

Chardin made his name with (@highlight art:drawer art:card_wall strength soft@) still lifes, near the bottom of the ladder: the French Academy put gods, saints and heroes at the top.

Around 1730, after a fellow painter teased him about his lowly subjects, he began to paint people: mostly women and children, at home.
    highlight art:boy

    erase chardin

    pause 1

So what is this boy really doing?
    highlight art:face lift true

    pause 1

At first glance, playing cards. But look at his hands.
    highlight art:hands effect detail

He isn't holding a hand to play. He folds each card down the middle, so that it can stand on its own.
    highlight art:folded effect detail

And at his fingertips, a row of folded cards stands like a little wall. He is building, not playing.
    highlight art:card_wall effect detail

    pause 1

Cards had another life, of course: on the gr (@highlight art:coins effect detail@)een cloth lie coins, as on any gaming table.

    ask "Tap it." spot jack on art
        intro: One card here has a figure on it.
        right: There it is, in the open drawer.
        wrong: Look below the table top, in the drawer.

A jack of hearts, the only picture ca (@highlight art:jack art:blank effect detail@)rd, and beside it, a blank one.

Some read the jack as a nod to the gamblers' world.
    highlight art:jack effect detail

The boy has no use for faces: to him, (@highlight art:card_wall effect detail@) a card is just a brick.

    pause 1

    camera on ["art:card_wall"] zoom 2.5

Prints of Chardin's card-builders were sold with a little verse, asking which is more solid: the child's castles, or our grown-up plans.
    highlight art:card_wall strength soft

That's the old moral: everything we build is a house of cards.
    point at.ref art:card_wall gesture glow

    pause 1

    camera reset true

But the painting itself doesn't preach. There is no motto, and no one wags a finger.
    highlight art:face strength soft lift true

    camera on ["art:face","art:hands"]

What Chardin paints instead is attention: eyes lowered, hands steady, the whole world shrunk to one card.
    focus art:face art:hands strength strong rest grey

    pause 1

    camera reset true

And he gives it the care others gave to saints: so (@highlight art:cheek effect detail@)ft light on the cheek,

the white cuff,
    highlight art:cuff effect detail

the worn wood of the drawer.
    highlight art:drawer_front effect detail

Like the milkmaid's pour, a moment of nothing much is held still, so that we notice it.
    highlight art:hands lift true

    pause 1

    camera reset true

Collectors wanted it: by 1774 this very canvas belonged to Catherine the Great of Russia.
    highlight art:boy strength soft

    camera on ["art:builder"]

So the boy is doing something serious, building with total concentration, and so is the painter, one slow stroke at a time.
    highlight art:builder rest paper lift true

    pause 1

    camera reset true

    quiz "A photographer wants an everyday moment worth a long look, the way Chardin does. Which shot follows his method?"
        right: The girl and her needle: no event, no message, just one quiet moment of attention, held.
        wrong: Chardin paints no big event and no message, only one quiet moment of focus.
        * A crowd cheering a goal
        * A crying child, with a caption about sadness
        * A toy tower, caught as it falls
        + A girl threading a needle, close, unhurried

Next time you see someone lost in a small task, look a little longer. Chardin did.
    highlight art:face lift true

